From 2431807e6c2044f7f6134aadf3680078dedb3a56 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shikosu <teovidal01@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 19:50:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] AArch64: pair exclusives, saturating narrows, FRECPE and
 FRECPX, half precision conversions, register-form CCMN

The instructions that real code still sent to the JIT (issue #16 of NX2Linux), each written from the
Arm Architecture Reference Manual and checked against Unicorn's AArch64 core (values, NZCV, memory and
the FPSR flags) by test_aot_simd.py and test_aot_atomics.py.

  * LDXP, LDAXP, STXP, STLXP (two W or two X registers). The monitor gets a third pseudo register,
    MONITOR_VALUE_HI (Arch::InitializeEmptyLiftedFunction), for the high word of a 128-bit pair. The
    pair is read as two words, not atomically: a torn read only makes the store-exclusive fail and the
    program retries. The store is one compare-and-swap of the pair's width against what the load read:
    64 bits for two W registers, 128 for two X registers (__remill_compare_exchange_memory_128, which the
    runtime does with cmpxchg16b).
  * SQXTN, UQXTN, SQXTUN, vector (with the "2" forms) and scalar, with the QC saturation flag.
  * FRECPE (single and double, vector and scalar) and FRECPX (scalar): the manual's FPRecipEstimate and
    RecipEstimate on the bits, with FPCR's rounding mode for the overflow case, and the invalid, divide by
    zero, overflow and inexact flags. FPCR.FZ is not modelled, as for FRSQRTE.
  * FCVT between half and single or double, FCVTN and FCVTL with 4H and 8H: FPConvert on the bits, with
    FPCR's rounding mode, IOC for a signalling NaN, OFC and IXC for an overflow, UFC and IXC for a tiny
    inexact result, IXC for any inexact one. FPCR.FZ16 and AHP are not modelled.
  * CCMN, register form (W and X): the semantics existed for the immediate form.

Not done, on purpose: the LSE atomics (CAS, SWP, LDADD and the others, ARMv8.1), which the Switch's
Cortex-A57 does not have and which only sit in outline-atomics code behind a test that fails there, and
the half-precision arithmetic (ARMv8.2), which that CPU does not have either. They stay in the JIT.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
